                <pb facs="0962" n="950" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg950"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">361.1121 Pyk, Irena Teodozja/10: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-03-31T15:00:00+03:00">March 31, 1941—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 4:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>636. As illustrating the difficulties inherent in obtaining the release of any
                    individual imprisoned in the Soviet Union, I have recently learned from a
                    reliable source that Molotov’s sister-in-law and Vyshinski’s daughter, his only
                    child, have been in jail for some months during the past year.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
